    "answer": {
      "short": "Correct answer: D.",
      "worked": "Adding an edge increases each endpoint's degree by 1, changing its parity. Joining the two odd-degree vertices leaves no odd-degree vertices. Joining an odd-degree vertex to an even-degree vertex leaves two odd-degree vertices. Joining two even-degree vertices gives four odd-degree vertices. Exactly one odd-degree vertex is therefore impossible.\n\nOption B is possible: a path through five vertices has degrees $1,2,2,2,1$. Joining its endpoints produces a cycle in which all five vertices have degree 2.\n\nCorrect answer: D.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
